an investigation is underway into the  death of a teenager who was on an  amusement park ride in orlando florida  the team fell on thursday night from an  attraction that's billed as the world's  tallest freestanding drop tower  there is no report of safety issues at  the ride is now closed indefinitely  manuel bajorkas reports  before the orlando freefall ride at icon  park lifted these riders one appeared to  question the seat restraints  the ride appears to have only an over  the shoulder harness that locks riders  into place as it descends 75 miles per  hour as it dropped for more than 400  feet a rider came off the seat and fell  to his death orange county sheriff  identified him as tyree sampson he was  14 years old he was visiting  from his home in missouri with another  family here in central florida this  photo published by the daily mail  reportedly shows sampson on the ride  john stein is with slingshot group which  owns the ride  we are deeply saddened and sorry he says  the ride which opened about three months  ago is designed to only operate if  riders are locked in what possibly could  have gone wrong it's very difficult to  say  the way the ride is designed with all  the safety features and redundancy there  shouldn't be an issue workers could be  heard after the incident  icon park says it's doing everything it  can to cooperate with authorities as  they investigate for cbs saturday  morning manuel bojorquez miami
 

